      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. They reported search relevance degradation starting on 2026-02-20 after the taxonomy change rollout, with saved searches returning poor and duplicate results, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to BlueHarbor Logistics to get them comfortable with renewal includes:\n\n- Updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer to map new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches.\n- Receiving a schema registry export and 14 days of anonymized query logs from the customer by 2026-03-19.\n- Starting an A/B test in staging on 2026-03-22 lasting 7-10 business days (5 days tuning + 2 days validation).\n- Validating that the top-5 correct hit rate is at least 80% for prioritized queries, with no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n- If validation passes, performing a 1-day production rollout followed by 72 hours of monitoring.\n- Offering a 90-day professional services engagement at a fixed price of $48,000, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator expansion is signed concurrently.\n- Including a conditional credit clause in the SOW addendum: if the proof does not meet the agreed accuracy uplift within 10 business days, BlueHarbor Logistics will receive a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal for the upcoming year.\n- Risk mitigation steps include fallback reindexing (~15 TB data, 48 hours compute, ~$25k cost) if mapping is insufficient, and discouragement of parallel use of NoiseGuard until proof completion.\n\nThese details and commercial terms were confirmed in the renewal negotiation call transcript and retro document [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nNo other customer issue starting after 2026-02-20 with a proposed proof plan for renewal comfort is documented in the provided evidence.",
