      "answer": "There is a recurring Canada approval-bypass pattern across multiple Canadian accounts after migration from legacy workflow tooling, not just a one-off at MapleBridge Insurance. The customers experiencing this recurring pattern include MapleBridge Insurance, MapleFork Franchise Systems, MapleWest Bank, MaplePath Career Institute, MapleBay Marketplace, Maple Regional Transit Authority, and City of Verdant Bay.\n\nThe shared failure pattern is that after migration, approval workflows for Canada-specific policies fail due to incorrect rule evaluation precedence: global-default or global rules override or bypass region-specific (country or province) approval rules. This causes approvals to be stuck, bypassed, auto-denied, or routed incorrectly. The root causes include rule evaluation order bugs, schema key mismatches (such as 'country_code' vs 'country'), delayed schema registry synchronization causing inconsistent rule sets across evaluation pods, and missing or ambiguous merge metadata leading to fallback to global default rules.\n\nIn plain English, after migrating to the new approval system, the system sometimes incorrectly applies global approval rules before or instead of Canada-specific rules. This causes approvals that should require Canadian regional approval to be bypassed, stuck, or auto-denied, leading to manual intervention, approval delays, or misrouted approvals.\n\nMapleBridge Insurance is a prominent example with a detailed incident involving about 117 approvals bypassed due to delayed schema registry sync causing some pods to use global.v3 rules instead of canada.v2, with a hotfix and override rule deployed as mitigation. Other customers like MapleFork Franchise Systems and MapleWest Bank have similar issues with region overrides ignored or global-default rules evaluated last, causing blocked or misrouted approvals.\n\nThis pattern is triggered by migration from legacy workflow tooling to Orchestrator, and evidence includes support tickets, technical validation calls, playbooks, and mitigation plans for these customers, all describing similar issues and fixes around rule precedence and schema normalization after migration.\n\nNo exact quantitative comparison of the frequency or scale of the approval-bypass pattern across all accounts is provided to definitively rank MapleBridge as a one-off or typical case; however, multiple accounts show similar issues. There is no explicit statement that MapleBridge is unique or isolated; evidence supports a recurring pattern across multiple customers but does not quantify relative severity or uniqueness beyond the detailed MapleBridge case [art_cbfb5f92862c, art_6be1b68b59cb, art_39c1434aa40a, art_f4a8c516b934, art_e9c20e0a23e0, art_981952a71434, art_b86a0ca2ce1e, art_ad58f3ce1afd, art_54e35e2cb431, art_3db205cb21ca, art_e697b3abe158, art_6376b15b1c33, art_4e17804e43b1].",
